"""Distinct patient topics retained following the November 2026 audit."""

def guide(slug, title, intro, body, source, related):
    return (f'/services/{slug}/', title, intro, body, source,
            'Read the patient information source', f'/services/{related}/')

ADA = 'https://www.mouthhealthy.org/all-topics-a-z/'
RETAINED_PAGES = [
guide('dental-checkups', 'Dental checkups in Edmonton',
      'A checkup looks at the health of your teeth and gums and helps you plan any care you need.',
      '''<h2>What happens at a checkup?</h2><p>A dental examination and a cleaning serve different purposes. During an examination, the dentist checks your teeth, gums and mouth, discusses symptoms, and considers whether further records or treatment are needed. A cleaning removes deposits from teeth. Your appointment may include both, but confirm what is booked.</p><h2>Will I need x-rays?</h2><p>X-rays are not automatically needed at every visit. Tell the office when and where you last had them taken so the dentist can decide whether existing images are useful. The decision depends on your history and examination.</p><h2>How often should I come back?</h2><p>The interval depends on your oral health and risk of problems. Before leaving, ask when your next examination and cleaning should be and whether they should happen together.</p><h2>Preparing for a first visit</h2><p>Bring a current medication list, insurance or CDCP details, and any questions about changes in your mouth. Let the office know about pain when you book; a routine checkup may not allow enough time to assess an urgent concern.</p><h2>What will it cost?</h2><p>The total depends on the examination, cleaning and records needed. Request an estimate before agreeing to care and ask which items your plan covers. Coverage is not a guarantee that there will be no balance to pay.</p>''',
      ADA+'x-rays', 'teeth-cleaning'),
guide('teeth-cleaning', 'Teeth cleaning and hygiene visits',
      'Understand what a hygiene appointment includes and how it differs from treatment for gum disease.',
      '''<h2>What does a cleaning do?</h2><p>Professional cleaning removes plaque and hardened deposits that brushing alone cannot remove. The visit also gives you a chance to discuss bleeding gums, difficult-to-clean areas, and your home routine.</p><h2>Is a cleaning the same as a checkup?</h2><p>No. A checkup assesses oral health; cleaning removes deposits. They can be booked together, but are separate parts of care. Ask what your appointment includes and whether the dentist will see you that day.</p><h2>What if my gums bleed or my teeth are sensitive?</h2><p>Tell the dental team before cleaning starts. Bleeding can be a sign of gum inflammation and deserves assessment. Gum disease may require care below the gum line and a different follow-up schedule from routine cleaning.</p><h2>How much time and money should I plan for?</h2><p>The amount of cleaning needed affects appointment length and cost. If it has been a while since your last visit, say so when booking. Ask for an estimate and whether additional visits could be needed.</p><h2>Between appointments</h2><p>Brush with fluoride toothpaste and clean between teeth daily. Ask the hygienist to demonstrate an approach that works around your bridges, braces or other dental work. Your return interval should reflect your needs, rather than a fixed schedule for everyone.</p>''',
      'https://www.nidcr.nih.gov/health-info/gum-disease', 'periodontal-disease-therapy'),
guide('dental-fillings-and-bonding', 'Dental fillings and bonding',
      'Fillings repair damaged tooth structure. Bonding can also change the shape or appearance of a tooth.',
      '''<h2>When is a filling used?</h2><p>A filling restores a tooth after decay or other damage has been assessed. The dentist considers how much healthy tooth remains, where the damage is and the forces on the tooth. A larger repair may need a crown instead.</p><h2>How is cosmetic bonding different?</h2><p>Bonding uses tooth-coloured material to make a repair or change a tooth's shape. It may be discussed for a small chip or a gap, but the bite and the health of the tooth matter. Cosmetic work does not replace treatment for decay or gum disease.</p><h2>Will I need freezing?</h2><p>This depends on the repair. Discuss comfort and sensitivity before treatment, especially if you have had difficulty with dental anaesthetic before. Ask what to expect afterward and when to call if discomfort continues.</p><h2>Bonding, veneers or a crown?</h2><p>These options preserve and cover different amounts of tooth. Bonding can be suitable for a smaller change; a veneer covers the front surface; a crown covers more of the tooth. An examination helps establish which options fit your situation.</p><h2>Costs and maintenance</h2><p>Ask how many teeth or surfaces are involved in the estimate and whether the work is restorative or cosmetic for insurance purposes. Repaired teeth still need brushing, cleaning between teeth and checkups. Report a sharp edge, a change in your bite, or a loose repair.</p>''',
      ADA+'dental-filling-options', 'dental-crowns'),
guide('dental-crowns', 'Dental crowns: repair and treatment planning',
      'A crown covers a tooth to restore its shape and function when a smaller repair may not be enough.',
      '''<h2>Why might a crown be recommended?</h2><p>A crown can support a weakened or broken tooth, restore a tooth with a large filling, or form part of care after root canal treatment. The dentist needs to assess the remaining tooth and its support before recommending one.</p><h2>What happens before treatment?</h2><p>The assessment comes first. Discuss alternatives, the amount of tooth preparation, the material, and any treatment needed before the crown. A crown cannot make every damaged tooth suitable to keep.</p><h2>How many appointments will I need?</h2><p>The schedule depends on the restoration and how it is made. Confirm the number of visits, whether a temporary crown is needed, and who to contact if it comes loose. Do not assume same-day treatment or a particular system is available.</p><h2>What is included in the estimate?</h2><p>Ask whether the estimate includes preparation, the crown, laboratory work and any separate treatment such as a buildup or root canal. Your insurer may require a proposed treatment plan before confirming benefits.</p><h2>Caring for a crown</h2><p>Clean around the gum line and between teeth each day. The tooth beneath a crown can still develop decay. Contact the office if the crown feels loose, your bite feels different, or discomfort persists.</p>''',
      ADA+'crowns', 'crowns-bridges'),
guide('dental-bridges', 'Dental bridges for missing teeth',
      'A bridge fills a gap left by missing teeth and is supported by teeth or implants.',
      '''<h2>How does a bridge work?</h2><p>A bridge joins replacement teeth to supports on either side of a gap. A conventional tooth-supported bridge usually requires preparation of the supporting teeth. The dentist checks those teeth, the gums and your bite before recommending it.</p><h2>Bridge, implant or denture?</h2><p>A bridge is fixed in place. A removable partial denture can replace missing teeth without the same design, while an implant-supported option uses support placed in the jaw. Each involves different treatment, cleaning and cost considerations. Not every option suits every mouth.</p><h2>What does treatment involve?</h2><p>Planning may include images and records, care for the supporting teeth, preparation and fitting. Ask whether you will have a temporary restoration, how many appointments are expected and what to do if it becomes loose.</p><h2>Cleaning under a bridge</h2><p>The replacement tooth and supporting teeth need daily cleaning. Ask the team to demonstrate a floss threader or another suitable aid for reaching beneath the bridge.</p><h2>Planning the cost</h2><p>The size of the bridge and the condition of its supports affect the plan. Request an itemised estimate, including any care required first, and check coverage before proceeding.</p>''',
      ADA+'bridges', 'dental-implants'),
guide('teeth-whitening', 'Teeth whitening: what to consider',
      'Whitening changes the colour of natural teeth. An examination helps explain what results are realistic.',
      '''<h2>Will whitening work on my teeth?</h2><p>Different causes of discolouration respond differently. Crowns, veneers and fillings do not whiten like natural teeth, so existing dental work can affect the final colour match.</p><h2>Before choosing a product</h2><p>Discuss tooth sensitivity, gum health and any visible restorations. Decay or other problems may need attention first. Tell the dentist about whitening products you already use and any irritation they have caused.</p><h2>What about sensitivity?</h2><p>Whitening can cause temporary sensitivity or irritation. Follow the instructions for the specific product and ask what to do if symptoms develop. More frequent use is not a substitute for following the recommended routine.</p><h2>Which treatment and how much?</h2><p>Ask which options are currently offered, what is included, and whether trays or follow-up are separate. Do not assume a particular brand or a fixed shade change. Discuss the total estimate and any limits of insurance coverage before starting.</p>''',
      ADA+'teeth-whitening', 'cosmetic-dentistry'),
guide('dental-veneers', 'Dental veneers: benefits and limits',
      'Veneers cover the front surface of teeth to change their appearance.',
      '''<h2>When might veneers be considered?</h2><p>Veneers may be discussed for changes to tooth shape or colour. The teeth and gums should be assessed first. Veneers do not treat underlying decay or gum disease, and the bite can affect suitability.</p><h2>What should I understand before deciding?</h2><p>Preparing a tooth for a veneer may involve removing enamel. Ask how much preparation is proposed, whether the change is reversible, and what future replacement could involve. Discuss bonding, whitening or orthodontics when those could address your concern.</p><h2>Planning treatment and costs</h2><p>The number of teeth, material and preparation affect the plan. Ask what the estimate includes, whether temporary restorations are needed, and how the proposed shape and colour will be agreed. Cosmetic care may not be covered by your plan.</p><h2>Aftercare</h2><p>Brush and clean between teeth daily, including around the edges of the veneers. Avoid using teeth to open packaging. Contact the office if a veneer chips, loosens or your bite feels uncomfortable; do not try to glue it back yourself.</p>''',
      ADA+'veneers', 'dental-fillings-and-bonding'),
guide('dental-braces', 'Braces and orthodontic choices',
      'Braces and clear aligners move teeth under a supervised treatment plan.',
      '''<h2>Braces or clear aligners?</h2><p>Fixed braces use brackets and wires; clear aligners use a series of removable appliances. Suitability depends on the movement needed, your bite and your ability to follow the treatment routine. An assessment is needed before choosing.</p><h2>How long does treatment take?</h2><p>There is no single timetable for every patient. The planned changes, oral health and progress at follow-up visits affect the schedule. Ask for a proposed range and what could extend it.</p><h2>What should an estimate cover?</h2><p>Ask about records, appliances, routine visits, repairs, refinements and retainers. Confirm whether treatment is provided at the clinic or through a referral and which appliance options are currently available.</p><h2>Cleaning and retention</h2><p>Brackets and wires need careful daily cleaning. Removable aligners also require a consistent cleaning and wear routine. After active treatment, retainers help maintain the result; ask about their wear schedule and replacement costs before starting.</p>''',
      ADA+'braces', 'orthodontics-invisalign'),
guide('gum-lift-procedure', 'Gum contouring and gum lift questions',
      'A change to the gum line should start with an assessment of gum health and the cause of the concern.',
      '''<h2>What is gum contouring?</h2><p>Gum contouring changes the shape of gum tissue around teeth. The terms gum lift and laser gum contouring can describe related procedures, but the instrument does not establish whether treatment is suitable.</p><h2>Cosmetic concern or gum disease?</h2><p>Gum swelling, recession and the amount of gum visible when smiling have different causes. They should not be treated as the same problem. An examination helps identify what is happening and whether gum disease needs attention first.</p><h2>Before agreeing to treatment</h2><p>Ask what tissue would be changed, whether the supporting bone is involved, and whether specialist assessment is appropriate. Discuss the intended result, healing, risks and alternatives. Confirm availability and the treating provider rather than assuming a laser procedure is offered.</p><h2>Estimate and recovery</h2><p>Request a written estimate and instructions specific to the proposed procedure. Ask when to return for review and who to call about bleeding, pain or other concerns. Cosmetic treatment may not be covered by insurance.</p>''',
      'https://www.nidcr.nih.gov/health-info/gum-disease', 'periodontal-disease-therapy'),
guide('infant-frenectomies', 'Infant tongue-tie and feeding concerns',
      'Feeding difficulties need an assessment of the baby and feeding process, not just the appearance of a tongue-tie.',
      '''<h2>Does every tongue-tie need treatment?</h2><p>No. Some babies with tongue-tie feed without difficulty. If feeding is painful, prolonged or ineffective, seek help from the baby's medical provider and a qualified feeding professional. An examination can help establish whether tongue movement is contributing.</p><h2>What happens before a procedure is considered?</h2><p>Assessment should consider feeding, growth and other possible causes of the difficulty. Feeding support may be part of the plan. A visible band of tissue alone does not establish that a frenectomy will help.</p><h2>Questions about a referral</h2><p>Tell the office your baby's age, the feeding concern and who referred you. Confirm whether infant assessment or treatment is currently available and who would provide it. Bring relevant referral notes and feeding assessments.</p><h2>Benefits, risks and follow-up</h2><p>Ask what current problem the procedure is intended to address, what improvement is realistic, what risks are involved, and what alternatives exist. Do not assume it will prevent future speech, dental or developmental problems. Arrange a clear follow-up and feeding-support plan with the treating team.</p>''',
      'https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/tongue-tie/', 'frenulopasty-and-frenectomy'),
]
